@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_NavGroup}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Accessibility}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_NavGroup @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_NavGroup @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</h1>

<p>
    This Nav Group component was built following the W3C ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG) for a simple disclosure pattern. By adhering to standardised patterns, we ensure that the component
    behaves predictably across different browsers and assistive technologies.
</p>
<p>
    In this instance, given the component is designed to be part of a vertical list structure, without obscuring any content when sections are expanded no additional keyboard support was added, and
    it only uses the standard keys such as Tab, Space and Enter.
</p>
<p>
    The container component, the NavGroup, has an <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">AriaLabelledBy</span> parameter that allows you to associate one or more elements by their <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">id</span> attribute to form the accessible name for the root <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">ul</span> element via its <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">aria-labelledby</span> attribute.
    This can be helpful for screen reader users as it gives a name and context to the list, which is announced when the list is entered. For example, on this site there is a "Components" heading in the side navigation whose <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">id</span> is associated with a NavGroup. When a screen reader enters the list, rather than simply announcing "list" it will announce "Components list".
</p>
<p>
    A unique <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">id</span> attribute is generated for each NavSection <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">button</span> element and is associated with its disclosed <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">ul</span> element via <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">aria-labelledby</span>. This means the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">Title</span> parameter value provided for the section becomes the accessible name for that nested list, giving screen reader users the same contextual announcement as they navigate deeper into the hierarchy.
</p>
<h2 id="nav-group-keyboard">Keyboard interaction</h2>
<ul aria-labelledby="nav-group-keyboard" class="keyboard-list">
    <li>
        <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Tab</kbd> - when focus moves into the Nav Group, places focus onto the the first focusable element which could be a link or a section (button for the disclosure).
    </li>
    <li><kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Shift + Tab</kbd> reverses tab direction.</li>
    <li><kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Space or Enter</kbd> - when on a section expands or collapses that section.</li>
    <li><kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Enter</kbd> - when on a link activates it.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="device-testing">Accessibility device testing</h2>

<ul aria-labelledby="device-testing">
    <li>JAWS, Narrator and NVDA on Windows were each paired with every one of the following browsers: Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge and Mozilla Firefox.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on macOS was paired with Apple's Safari.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on iPhone was paired with mobile Safari.</li>
    <li>Talkback on Android was paired with mobile Google Chrome.</li>
    <li>Voice access on Windows 11</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong> Testing was performed with the latest browser and assistive technologies versions that were available at the time of testing (see release history on the installation page for approximate testing dates)
</p>
<h2 id="screen-reader-quirks">Screen reader quirks or known issues</h2>
<ul aria-labelledby="screen-reader-quirks">
    <li>
        <b>Narrator</b> more often than not will not announce that the section item has been expanded after activation when
        parried with either Edge or Chrome, but is fine when paired with Firefox.
    </li>
    <li>
        <b>JAWS</b> paired with Firefox will sometimes repeat section names and actions (expanded/collapsed).
    </li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong> The same quirks were also noted in the ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG) disclosure examples.
</p>
<div class="flex-content justify-content-between">
    <a class="@GlobalValues.Link_Button_Class @GlobalValues.Link_Button_Left_Arrow_Modifier" href="@($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_NavGroup}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Installation}")">
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_NavGroup - @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Installation</span>
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </a>
    <a class="@GlobalValues.Link_Button_Class @GlobalValues.Link_Button_Right_Arrow_Modifier" href="@($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_NavGroup}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Api}")">
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_NavGroup - @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Api</span>
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
    </a>
</div>
